ACADEMY.reg("cap-008.en", {"id": "cap-008", "num": 8, "title": "Six Sigma Basic Concepts", "part": "PARTE I — Fundamentos da Qualidade e do Six Sigma", "html": "<h3 id=\"standard-deviation\">Standard Deviation</h3>\n<p>Six Sigma's core goal is reducing defects. By reducing defects, teams can increase productivity, cut overall costs, boost customer satisfaction, and generate maximum profit. An idea inherent to the Six Sigma methodology is that variance is the root of many defects.</p>\n<p>For example, if an oven heats to exactly 350 degrees within five minutes and stays at that temperature until it's turned off, it's less likely to burn the cookies. If a cook measures every ingredient exactly, they're more likely to produce cookies that consistently taste good. Add variation to the process, and consistency is lost. When consistency is lost, defects get introduced. If the oven doesn't hold an exact temperature the whole time, the cookies might burn. If the cook puts in one cup of sugar instead of one and a half, the cookies might not be sweet enough. Variation leads to inconsistent quality.</p>\n<p>It's important to note that simply removing variation doesn't always improve quality. What if the cook set the oven to 400 degrees every time and used only half a cup of sugar for every batch? The process has no variation, and neither do the results. The cookies will always be bland and burnt.</p>\n<p>Six Sigma process improvement teams generally take a two-step approach to improvements. First, they need to determine whether the process is functional. In the cookie example, does the recipe work? Is there even a recipe? Once the team determines a viable recipe exists, they make improvements to remove the variation that causes results to drift from the recipe's intended outcome.</p>\n<p>The statistical measure teams use to understand variation in a process is known as standard deviation. Standard deviation is represented mathematically by the lowercase Greek letter sigma — the σ you saw in Chapter 2.</p>\n<p>Standard deviation measures the distance between data points and the mean of all the data. A large standard deviation means a wide spread of points; a smaller standard deviation means a more tightly clustered set of points.</p>\n<p><em>Figure: on the left, data points clustered close to the mean (\"less deviation\"); on the right, spread-out data points (\"more deviation\").</em></p>\n<p>The image above provides a graphical representation of deviation. Imagine the vertical axis is a measure of time and the horizontal axis is a measure of temperature. The center line in each image represents the average temperature. You can see the temperature over time varies much more in the figure on the right.</p>\n<h4 id=\"calculating-standard-deviation-for-population-data\">Calculating Standard Deviation for Population Data</h4>\n<p>Standard deviation is a statistical concept. The formula for standard deviation when dealing with data for an entire population is:</p>\n<p><strong>σ = √[ (1/N) × Σ(xᵢ − μ)² ]</strong>, for i = 1 through N</p>\n<p><strong>Formula Key:</strong>\n- σ = Standard deviation\n- μ = mean\n- Σ indicates you should add up the results of every calculation done for the items listed in parentheses\n- N = the number of data elements you calculated the standard deviation for\n- X = a placeholder for each data element</p>\n<p>You'd use this formula if you had every data element for a population, not just a random sample of data elements. For example, if you wanted to find out the deviation in the size of the pizzas produced, you could ask the team to measure every pizza before serving it. You'd have the data for the entire day's population of pizzas, so you could use the equation above. However, if you wanted to calculate standard deviation when you have sample data, you'd use the equation in the next section.</p>\n<p>If you're new to statistics, the standard deviation equation looks complicated. We'll break it down and practice a few manual standard-deviation calculation exercises, but in practice, you'll generally use a statistical software tool to run this calculation automatically.</p>\n<p>For our explanation, we'll use a data set from a teacher. She wants to find out the standard deviation of the grades on the last test. Her class of 15 students' grades are:</p>\n<p>67, 68, 73, 74, 81, 85, 88, 88, 90, 90, 90, 93, 94, 98, 99</p>\n<p><strong>1. Calculate the mean.</strong></p>\n<p>To start the standard deviation calculation, you need to know the population's mean. The mean is represented mathematically by the Greek letter mu, or μ. The mean is calculated by adding up all the numbers and dividing that sum by the number of items in a data set. In this case, there are 15 items.</p>\n<p>67 + 68 + 73 + 74 + 81 + 85 + 88 + 88 + 90 + 90 + 90 + 93 + 94 + 98 + 99 = 1278</p>\n<p>1278/15 = 85.2</p>\n<p><strong>2. Subtract the mean and square the result.</strong></p>\n<p>The formula requires you to take each number in the data set, subtract the mean from it, and square the result. The first number is 67, so:</p>\n<p>67 − 85.2 = −18.2</p>\n<p>−18.2 × −18.2 = 331.24</p>\n<p>If you apply this concept to all 15 numbers, you'll have a list of results:</p>\n<p>331.24; 295.84; 148.84; 125.44; 17.64; 0.04; 7.84; 7.84; 23.04; 23.04; 23.04; 60.84; 77.44; 163.84; 190.44</p>\n<p><strong>3. Find the mean of the results.</strong></p>\n<p>The rest of the formula under the square root sign simply tells you to add up all the numbers you just calculated and divide by N, where N is the number of items in your data set. Or, in other words, you need to find the mean of the new numbers you just calculated.</p>\n<p>The sum of the numbers above is 1496.4.</p>\n<p>1496.4 / 15 = 99.76</p>\n<p>This new number, 99.76, is called the variance.</p>\n<p><strong>4. Find the square root of the variance.</strong></p>\n<p>Standard deviation is the square root of the variance. In this case, the square root of 99.76, which is 9.987.</p>\n<p>The standard deviation of the test grades is 9.987.</p>\n<h4 id=\"calculating-standard-deviation-with-sample-data\">Calculating Standard Deviation with Sample Data</h4>\n<p>Although statistics based on full population data are always more accurate than those based on sample data, you'll likely work with sample data more often. It simply becomes too expensive, or even impossible, to get population data for many elements. Sometimes data measures events or states over time, which means population data doesn't exist. For example, if you wanted to understand temperature fluctuations in a warehouse, you could log the temperature at a certain location every ten minutes. After several days, you have enough sample data to analyze.</p>\n<p>Other examples of sample data include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A random sample of reasons for denied medical claims</li>\n<li>River level measurements taken three times a day over a month</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The formula for standard deviation based on sample data is:</p>\n<p><strong>S = √[ (1/(N−1)) × Σ(xᵢ − x̄)² ]</strong>, for i = 1 through N</p>\n<p>Since mu is the mean of the population data, it's been replaced in this formula by x-bar, which is the mean of the data points in your sample. Sigma has been replaced by s, but the only mathematical difference is that you divide by N−1 instead of N to get the variance, as a way to compensate for some of the precision lost by using a sample.</p>\n<p>Using the same data from the population example above, let's assume the 15 grades the teacher had were a random sample from all her classes. The only difference in the math would be in the second-to-last step, where we divide by 14 instead of 15, so:</p>\n<p>1496.4 / 14 = 106.885</p>\n<p>The square root of 106.885 is 10.338, which would be the sample's standard deviation.</p>\n<h4 id=\"standard-deviation-in-excel\">Standard Deviation in Excel</h4>\n<p>Granted, if you're calculating standard deviation by hand, there's a lot of arithmetic involved. Fortunately, once the statistical concepts behind the numbers are understood, statistical analysis software, like Excel and Minitab, can be used to crunch the numbers accurately. Standard deviation is calculated automatically in most statistical analysis programs by clicking a button after entering your data sets. Standard deviation is also automatically calculated by these programs when you kick off other calculations that require standard deviation. We'll look at some of these functions further in the chapters on using Excel and Minitab add-ins for statistical analysis.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>See for yourself:</strong>\nLab technicians are measuring bacteria's response to an ingredient in a possible treatment. They want to know how long it takes for the bacteria to show a response. The sample data for the response times, in minutes, is:\n2; 3.5; 2.3; 2; 2.5; 3.1; 2.2; 3.2; 4\nCalculate the standard deviation.</p>\n<p><strong>Formula Key:</strong>\ns = Standard deviation of a sample\nx-bar = the sample mean\nΣ indicates you should add up the results of every calculation done for the items listed in parentheses\nN = the number of data elements you calculated the standard deviation for\nX = a placeholder for each data element\n</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>In Excel 365, standard deviation is calculated with a single formula. Type the data set into a column, then:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>In an empty cell, type <code>=STDEV.S(</code>, select the range with the data (e.g., A1:A9), and close the parenthesis</li>\n<li>Press Enter to get the result</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"figure2026\">\n<div style=\"background:#eef4f6;border:1px solid #cedde1;border-radius:8px;padding:14px 16px 18px;max-width:518px;margin:0.6rem auto;\">\n<svg aria-label=\"Sample standard deviation calculation in Excel 365 with STDEV.S\" role=\"img\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto;display:block;\" viewBox=\"0 0 470 300\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n<rect fill=\"#f8fbfb\" height=\"34\" rx=\"5\" stroke=\"#cedde1\" width=\"438\" x=\"16\" y=\"16\"></rect>\n<text fill=\"#00688f\" font-family=\"Segoe UI, sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" x=\"28\" y=\"38\">fx</text>\n<text fill=\"#132e3a\" font-family=\"Consolas, monospace\" font-size=\"13\" x=\"54\" y=\"38\">=STDEV.S(A1:A9)</text>\n<rect fill=\"#e2ecee\" height=\"24\" stroke=\"#c7d8dc\" width=\"70\" x=\"16\" y=\"40\"></rect>\n<text fill=\"#132e3a\" font-family=\"Segoe UI, sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"51\" y=\"56\">A</text>\n<rect fill=\"#e2ecee\" height=\"24\" stroke=\"#c7d8dc\" width=\"120\" x=\"96\" y=\"40\"></rect>\n<text fill=\"#132e3a\" font-family=\"Segoe UI, sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"156\" y=\"56\">B</text>\n<rect fill=\"#f8fbfb\" height=\"24\" stroke=\"#cedde1\" width=\"70\" x=\"16\" y=\"64\"></rect><text fill=\"#26343a\" font-family=\"Consolas, monospace\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"51\" y=\"80\">2</text><rect fill=\"#f8fbfb\" height=\"24\" stroke=\"#cedde1\" width=\"70\" x=\"16\" y=\"88\"></rect><text fill=\"#26343a\" font-family=\"Consolas, monospace\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"51\" y=\"104\">3.5</text><rect fill=\"#f8fbfb\" height=\"24\" stroke=\"#cedde1\" width=\"70\" x=\"16\" y=\"112\"></rect><text fill=\"#26343a\" font-family=\"Consolas, monospace\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"51\" y=\"128\">2.3</text><rect fill=\"#f8fbfb\" height=\"24\" stroke=\"#cedde1\" width=\"70\" x=\"16\" y=\"136\"></rect><text fill=\"#26343a\" font-family=\"Consolas, monospace\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"51\" y=\"152\">2</text><rect fill=\"#f8fbfb\" height=\"24\" stroke=\"#cedde1\" width=\"70\" x=\"16\" y=\"160\"></rect><text fill=\"#26343a\" font-family=\"Consolas, monospace\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"51\" y=\"176\">2.5</text><rect fill=\"#f8fbfb\" height=\"24\" stroke=\"#cedde1\" width=\"70\" x=\"16\" y=\"184\"></rect><text fill=\"#26343a\" font-family=\"Consolas, monospace\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"51\" y=\"200\">3.1</text><rect fill=\"#f8fbfb\" height=\"24\" stroke=\"#cedde1\" width=\"70\" x=\"16\" y=\"208\"></rect><text fill=\"#26343a\" font-family=\"Consolas, monospace\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"51\" y=\"224\">2.2</text><rect fill=\"#f8fbfb\" height=\"24\" stroke=\"#cedde1\" width=\"70\" x=\"16\" y=\"232\"></rect><text fill=\"#26343a\" font-family=\"Consolas, monospace\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"51\" y=\"248\">3.2</text><rect fill=\"#f8fbfb\" height=\"24\" stroke=\"#cedde1\" width=\"70\" x=\"16\" y=\"256\"></rect><text fill=\"#26343a\" font-family=\"Consolas, monospace\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"51\" y=\"272\">4</text>\n<rect fill=\"#f8fbfb\" height=\"24\" stroke=\"#00688f\" stroke-width=\"2\" width=\"120\" x=\"96\" y=\"64\"></rect>\n<text fill=\"#00688f\" font-family=\"Consolas, monospace\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"156\" y=\"80\">0.7</text>\n<text fill=\"#26343a\" font-family=\"Segoe UI, sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" x=\"16\" y=\"270\">Sample: <tspan font-family=\"Consolas, monospace\">=STDEV.S()</tspan>   |   Population: <tspan font-family=\"Consolas, monospace\">=STDEV.P()</tspan></text>\n<text fill=\"#52646b\" font-family=\"Segoe UI, sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" x=\"16\" y=\"290\">Portuguese Excel: DESVPAD.A() for sample, DESVPAD.P() for population.</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n<figcaption>Schematic illustration of Excel 365: sample standard deviation calculation with STDEV.S, using the response times from the exercise above.</figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<p><em>Note: <code>STDEV.S</code> calculates the sample standard deviation (N−1 math); use <code>STDEV.P</code> when you have data for the entire population (N math). In Portuguese Excel, the equivalent functions are <code>DESVPAD.A</code> and <code>DESVPAD.P</code>. The old <code>STDEV()</code>/<code>STDEVP()</code> still work for compatibility, but Excel 365 recommends the <code>.S</code>/<code>.P</code> suffixed versions because they make explicit whether the calculation is for a sample or a population — an important distinction for any Six Sigma analysis.</em></p>\n<h4 id=\"why-calculate-standard-deviation\">Why Calculate Standard Deviation?</h4>\n<p>Standard deviation gives you a sense of how much variation actually exists in a process, accounting, to some extent, for outliers. In the grades example above, the sample standard deviation indicates most grades will fall within 10.33 points on either side of the mean.</p>\n<p>That tells the teacher that students performed fairly variably on her test. If the results were an average grade of 90 with a standard deviation of 3, she could assume the students in the class were performing pretty consistently with each other, learning and retaining the knowledge as expected. If the average grade were 64 with a standard deviation of 2, she could assume the students in the class weren't retaining the knowledge as expected, or that there was some problem with the test's structure. Both situations indicate small variance in how the students are performing, which points to the success or problem being tied to the class, the teaching, or the test.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, if the average grade were 60 with a standard deviation of 30, then some students were performing very well while others performed poorly. That could indicate to the teacher that some students are falling behind. If she gathered samples across several classes, she might investigate and find the lower grades were mostly from one class, which could indicate she forgot to properly cover a certain concept in that class.</p>\n<p>Standard deviation on its own serves as an indicator of where to investigate within the process for problems or solutions. Another reason to calculate it is that it's involved in many of the other statistical processes we'll cover in later chapters. Standard deviation becomes an important concept in both statistical analysis and statistical process control, and often serves as the starting point for deeper Six Sigma statistical analysis.</p>\n<p><img alt=\"Menos desvio vs. mais desvio\" src=\"assets/img/a4a5ba7c00e899ad.svg\" style=\"max-width:100%;\" /></p>\n<h4 id=\"worked-example-across-five-approaches-standard-deviation\">Worked example across five approaches: Standard Deviation</h4>\n<p>Picking up the <strong>\"See for yourself\"</strong> exercise above: lab technicians measured bacteria's response time to a possible treatment, in minutes, for a sample of 9 measurements:</p>\n<p><code>2; 3.5; 2.3; 2; 2.5; 3.1; 2.2; 3.2; 4</code></p>\n<p>Below, the same calculation — sample and population standard deviation — worked out in Excel, Power BI (DAX), Python, R, and Minitab. All five should converge on the same value (≈0.72 sample, ≈0.68 population).</p>\n<p><img alt=\"Ilustracao Excel + Power BI (DAX) do exemplo: Desvio Padrão\" src=\"assets/img/7918fa0c88f7550e.svg\" style=\"max-width:100%;\" /></p>\n<p><strong>Excel 365</strong> (formula already shown in the figure above, repeated here for quick reference): data in column A1:A9.</p>\n<pre><code>=STDEV.S(A1:A9)   ' sample (N-1) -&gt; 0.72\n=STDEV.P(A1:A9)   ' population (N)  -&gt; 0.68\n</code></pre>\n<p><em>(In Portuguese Excel: <code>DESVPAD.A</code> and <code>DESVPAD.P</code>, same formulas.)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Power BI (DAX)</strong> — a measure over a <code>ResponseTimes[Minutes]</code> table with the 9 values:</p>\n<div class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"dax\"><div class=\"codebar\"><span class=\"lang\">DAX</span><button class=\"btn-copy\" type=\"button\">Copiar</button></div><pre><code class=\"language-dax\">Sample Standard Deviation = STDEV.S ( TemposResposta[Minutos] )\nPopulation Standard Deviation = STDEV.P ( TemposResposta[Minutos] )\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>These two measures exist in DAX under the same function-family names as Excel (<code>STDEV.S</code>/<code>STDEV.P</code>) and can be dropped straight into a report card — one of the cases where Power BI calculates, not just visualizes.</p>\n<div class=\"codetabs\"><div class=\"ct-bar\" role=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ct-tab on\" role=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\" id=\"ct26830395-t0\">Python</button></div><div class=\"ct-pane\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"ct26830395-t0\"><div class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"python\"><div class=\"codebar\"><span class=\"lang\">Python</span><button class=\"btn-copy\" type=\"button\">Copiar</button></div><pre><code class=\"language-python\">import numpy as np\n\ndados = [2, 3.5, 2.3, 2, 2.5, 3.1, 2.2, 3.2, 4]\n\nmedia = np.mean(dados)\ndp_amostral = np.std(dados, ddof=1)      # N-1, equivalente a DESVPAD.A / STDEV.S\ndp_populacional = np.std(dados, ddof=0)  # N,   equivalente a DESVPAD.P / STDEV.P\n\nprint(f\"Média:                {media:.4f}\")\nprint(f\"Desvio padrão amostral (N-1):     {dp_amostral:.4f}\")\nprint(f\"Desvio padrão populacional (N):   {dp_populacional:.4f}\")</code></pre></div></div></div>\n<div class=\"codetabs\"><div class=\"ct-bar\" role=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ct-tab on\" role=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\" id=\"ct89376531-t0\">Python</button></div><div class=\"ct-pane\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"ct89376531-t0\"><div class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"python\"><div class=\"codebar\"><span class=\"lang\">Python</span><button class=\"btn-copy\" type=\"button\">Copiar</button></div><pre><code class=\"language-python\">%%R\ndados &lt;- c(2, 3.5, 2.3, 2, 2.5, 3.1, 2.2, 3.2, 4)\n\nmedia &lt;- mean(dados)\ndp_amostral &lt;- sd(dados)  # sd() do R ja usa N-1, equivalente a DESVPAD.A / STDEV.S\ndp_populacional &lt;- sqrt(mean((dados - media)^2))  # N, equivalente a DESVPAD.P / STDEV.P\n\ncat(sprintf(\"Media:                          %.4f\\n\", media))\ncat(sprintf(\"Desvio padrao amostral (N-1):   %.4f\\n\", dp_amostral))\ncat(sprintf(\"Desvio padrao populacional (N): %.4f\\n\", dp_populacional))</code></pre></div></div></div>\n<p><strong>Minitab</strong> (current version — check minitab.com): paste the 9 values into column C1, then <strong>Stat → Basic Statistics → Display Descriptive Statistics</strong> → select C1 → under <em>Statistics</em>, check <em>Standard deviation</em> → OK. Minitab reports the <strong>sample</strong> standard deviation by default (the same convention as <code>STDEV.S</code>/<code>DESVPAD.A</code>). Alternatively, <code>Calc → Column Statistics → Standard deviation</code> gives you the same number directly in the session worksheet.</p>\n<p><em>Verification note: all five methods should converge on the same value — here, sample standard deviation ≈0.7196 and population ≈0.6784. Excel, DAX, and Minitab are checked manually against the output printed by the Python and R cells above.</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"the-pareto-principle\">The Pareto Principle</h3>\n<p>The Pareto principle, also called the 80/20 rule, says 20% of the causes lead to 80% of the effects. This is also called the law of the vital few: the few vital inputs drive most of the results.</p>\n<p>The Pareto principle was first suggested by a management consultant named Joseph Juran. Juran named the principle after Vilfredo Pareto, an Italian economist who wrote that 20% of a nation's population owned 80% of its land. The principle became commonplace across various circles. Business professionals commonly state that 80% of sales come from 20% of customers, and volunteer organizations often operate with 20% of the people doing 80% of the work.</p>\n<p>The principle is fundamental to Six Sigma not because causes and effects line up perfectly on an 80/20 split, but because it's almost universally true that a few inputs create more impact than all the other inputs combined. Individuals looking to reduce defects can almost always identify three or four inputs that, if improved, will create a dramatic impact on the outcome. Although resources, costs, and the difficulty of improvements also play a role in selecting solutions, understanding which inputs or root causes sit at the top of a Pareto chart lets project teams determine where improvements will have the biggest impact on the bottom line.</p>\n<p>The Pareto principle is best displayed using a Pareto chart, which is a graphical representation of data elements — usually inputs or causes — on a ranked bar chart. Unlike a regular bar chart, the bars are arranged in order of height, with the tallest on the left and the shortest on the right. Statistical software used to build these charts adds formatting and other elements automatically, but you can also build a basic Pareto chart in Excel.</p>\n<p>To illustrate the Pareto principle, we'll look at a common situation involving defects in the medical field — specifically in the medical claim submission process. Insurers frequently deny claims, and they do so for a variety of reasons. When claims are denied, provider offices need to rework, resubmit, or appeal the denials. Some denial reasons aren't appealable, which means the office loses the revenue tied to the claim.</p>\n<p>Let's imagine a medical office that's facing a cash-flow problem because of claim denials. The office collects data on the denials and builds a Pareto chart so the team can see where most of the denials are coming from. The data is listed below, followed by a basic Pareto chart built in Excel.</p>\n<p><strong>Reasons for Medical Claim Denial</strong></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Reason</th>\n<th>Count</th>\n<th>%</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Duplicate claim</td>\n<td>18,012</td>\n<td>35.9%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Timely Filing</td>\n<td>13,245</td>\n<td>26.4%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Beneficiary not found</td>\n<td>10,215</td>\n<td>20.4%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Claim missing sufficient information</td>\n<td>4,548</td>\n<td>9.1%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Service not covered</td>\n<td>2,154</td>\n<td>4.3%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Medical necessity</td>\n<td>1,423</td>\n<td>2.8%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Date-of-service issue</td>\n<td>526</td>\n<td>1.0%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n\n<p>From the Pareto chart, you can see the top three denial reasons account for 80% of the denied claims. An experienced billing team could tell you three things just by looking at this data:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The office has rework muda. They're submitting a large percentage of claims more than once.</li>\n<li>The office has an efficiency problem. Nearly a quarter of its claims aren't reaching the insurer before filing deadlines.</li>\n<li>The office has an insurance-verification problem, because a fifth of its claims are being submitted with information that doesn't match anything on the insurer's end.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Handling the duplicate claims matters because it reduces rework and can improve the office's relationship with insurers. However, the team might choose to work on the filing-deadline problem first, because filing-deadline denials are final, meaning the office is losing the revenue tied to all those claims. Submitting claims on time isn't hard in most cases, given that most insurers allow months or even a year for claim submission, so this could be an \"easy\" win for the team. A Pareto chart often reveals obvious opportunities this way.</p>\n<h4 id=\"building-a-pareto-chart-in-excel-365\">Building a Pareto Chart in Excel 365</h4>\n<p>Excel 365 has a native Pareto chart, so you no longer have to manually build the cumulative-percentage columns to get the chart. Use the claim-denial data, or your own data, to practice:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Arrange the label column (Reason) and the value column (Count or %) side by side.</li>\n<li>Select the range with both columns.</li>\n<li>Go to <strong>Insert → Charts → Insert Statistic Chart → Histogram → Pareto</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"figure2026\">\n<div style=\"background:#eef4f6;border:1px solid #cedde1;border-radius:8px;padding:16px;max-width:760px;margin:0.6rem auto;\">\n<svg aria-label=\"Flow for building a native Pareto chart in Excel 365\" role=\"img\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto;display:block;\" viewBox=\"0 0 900 230\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n<rect fill=\"#f8fbfb\" height=\"150\" rx=\"8\" stroke=\"#cedde1\" width=\"240\" x=\"10\" y=\"30\"></rect>\n<text fill=\"#132e3a\" font-family=\"Segoe UI, sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"130\" y=\"55\">1. Select data</text>\n<rect fill=\"#e2ecee\" height=\"20\" stroke=\"#c7d8dc\" width=\"90\" x=\"35\" y=\"70\"></rect>\n<rect fill=\"#e2ecee\" height=\"20\" stroke=\"#c7d8dc\" width=\"60\" x=\"125\" y=\"70\"></rect>\n<text fill=\"#132e3a\" font-family=\"Segoe UI, sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"80\" y=\"84\">Reason</text>\n<text fill=\"#132e3a\" font-family=\"Segoe UI, sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"155\" y=\"84\">%</text>\n<rect fill=\"#f8fbfb\" height=\"18\" stroke=\"#cedde1\" width=\"90\" x=\"35\" y=\"90\"></rect>\n<rect fill=\"#f8fbfb\" height=\"18\" stroke=\"#cedde1\" width=\"60\" x=\"125\" y=\"90\"></rect>\n<rect fill=\"#f8fbfb\" height=\"18\" stroke=\"#cedde1\" width=\"90\" x=\"35\" y=\"108\"></rect>\n<rect fill=\"#f8fbfb\" height=\"18\" stroke=\"#cedde1\" width=\"60\" x=\"125\" y=\"108\"></rect>\n<rect fill=\"#f8fbfb\" height=\"18\" stroke=\"#cedde1\" width=\"90\" x=\"35\" y=\"126\"></rect>\n<rect fill=\"#f8fbfb\" height=\"18\" stroke=\"#cedde1\" width=\"60\" x=\"125\" y=\"126\"></rect>\n<rect fill=\"none\" height=\"84\" stroke=\"#00688f\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\" stroke-width=\"2\" width=\"160\" x=\"30\" y=\"65\"></rect>\n<text fill=\"#52646b\" font-family=\"Segoe UI, sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"130\" y=\"165\">sorted highest to lowest</text>\n<line marker-end=\"url(#arrow-u01-pareto)\" stroke=\"#00688f\" stroke-width=\"2\" x1=\"255\" x2=\"295\" y1=\"105\" y2=\"105\"></line>\n<rect fill=\"#f8fbfb\" height=\"180\" rx=\"8\" stroke=\"#cedde1\" width=\"270\" x=\"300\" y=\"15\"></rect>\n<text fill=\"#132e3a\" font-family=\"Segoe UI, sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"435\" y=\"38\">2. Excel 365 path</text>\n<rect fill=\"#e2ecee\" height=\"26\" rx=\"4\" stroke=\"#c7d8dc\" width=\"240\" x=\"315\" y=\"50\"></rect>\n<text fill=\"#132e3a\" font-family=\"Segoe UI, sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"435\" y=\"67\">Insert</text>\n<rect fill=\"#e2ecee\" height=\"26\" rx=\"4\" stroke=\"#c7d8dc\" width=\"240\" x=\"315\" y=\"84\"></rect>\n<text fill=\"#132e3a\" font-family=\"Segoe UI, sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"435\" y=\"101\">Statistic Chart</text>\n<rect fill=\"#e2ecee\" height=\"26\" rx=\"4\" stroke=\"#c7d8dc\" width=\"240\" x=\"315\" y=\"118\"></rect>\n<text fill=\"#132e3a\" font-family=\"Segoe UI, sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"435\" y=\"135\">Histogram</text>\n<rect fill=\"#00688f\" height=\"26\" rx=\"4\" stroke=\"#00688f\" width=\"240\" x=\"315\" y=\"152\"></rect>\n<text fill=\"#f8fbfb\" font-family=\"Segoe UI, sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"700\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"435\" y=\"169\">Pareto</text>\n<line marker-end=\"url(#arrow-u01-pareto)\" stroke=\"#00688f\" stroke-width=\"2\" x1=\"575\" x2=\"615\" y1=\"105\" y2=\"105\"></line>\n<rect fill=\"#f8fbfb\" height=\"150\" rx=\"8\" stroke=\"#cedde1\" width=\"270\" x=\"620\" y=\"30\"></rect>\n<text fill=\"#132e3a\" font-family=\"Segoe UI, sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"755\" y=\"52\">3. Automatic result</text>\n<rect fill=\"#00688f\" height=\"10\" width=\"18\" x=\"650\" y=\"150\"></rect>\n<rect fill=\"#00688f\" height=\"20\" width=\"18\" x=\"675\" y=\"140\"></rect>\n<rect fill=\"#00688f\" height=\"35\" width=\"18\" x=\"700\" y=\"125\"></rect>\n<rect fill=\"#00688f\" height=\"60\" width=\"18\" x=\"725\" y=\"100\"></rect>\n<polyline fill=\"none\" points=\"659,95 684,80 709,66 734,60\" stroke=\"#132e3a\" stroke-width=\"2\"></polyline>\n<circle cx=\"659\" cy=\"95\" fill=\"#132e3a\" r=\"3\"></circle>\n<circle cx=\"684\" cy=\"80\" fill=\"#132e3a\" r=\"3\"></circle>\n<circle cx=\"709\" cy=\"66\" fill=\"#132e3a\" r=\"3\"></circle>\n<circle cx=\"734\" cy=\"60\" fill=\"#132e3a\" r=\"3\"></circle>\n<text fill=\"#52646b\" font-family=\"Segoe UI, sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"755\" y=\"172\">bars + cumulative %</text>\n<defs>\n<marker id=\"arrow-u01-pareto\" markerheight=\"8\" markerwidth=\"8\" orient=\"auto\" refx=\"6\" refy=\"3\">\n<path d=\"M0,0 L6,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#00688f\"></path>\n</marker>\n</defs>\n</svg>\n</div>\n<figcaption>Excel 365 flow: select the data, open Insert &gt; Statistic Chart &gt; Histogram &gt; Pareto, and Excel generates the sorted bars with the cumulative-percentage line automatically.</figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<p>Excel sorts the bars from biggest to smallest cause and draws the cumulative-percentage line automatically — the same two elements that, in older versions, had to be built manually with a running-count column and a separately added trendline. To adjust data labels, the title, or the secondary percentage axis, click the chart and use the <strong>Chart Elements</strong> button (the \"+\" icon that appears next to the selected chart) or the <strong>Chart Design</strong> tab on the ribbon.</p>\n<p><em>Behind the scenes, the Pareto chart is still exactly what was described above: a sorted count by cause, with a cumulative-percentage line. Understanding this mechanic remains useful even when using the native tool, because it's what lets you correctly interpret the chart — for example, knowing at what point the cumulative line crosses 80%.</em></p>\n<h4 id=\"uses-for-pareto-charts\">Uses for Pareto Charts</h4>\n<p>Pareto charts are useful analytical tools when you need to analyze frequencies or causes of problems. They also help narrow down an approach to a problem that has many causes or is too broad to tackle in a single improvement project. As in the claim-denial example above, you might find a single cause to work on that can generate huge results across the whole process.</p>\n<p>Pareto charts are also useful when communicating information about causes to others, especially those outside the Six Sigma process. Although Pareto charts are a powerful analytical tool, they also present complex data in a visual format that's familiar to practically anyone. Business professionals know how to read a bar chart, and putting the chart in order just makes it easier for individuals to see the true causes behind problems. For that reason, many Six Sigma specialists regularly include Pareto charts when presenting to business leaders and others, especially if the data could be considered surprising or needs visual reinforcement.</p>\n<h4 id=\"worked-example-across-five-approaches-pareto-chart\">Worked example across five approaches: Pareto Chart</h4>\n<p>Picking up the <strong>Reasons for Medical Claim Denial</strong> data presented above (medical office, claim denials by reason). Below, the same Pareto chart — sorted count + cumulative percentage — worked out in Excel, Power BI (DAX), Python, R, and Minitab.</p>\n<p><img alt=\"Ilustracao Excel + Power BI (DAX) do exemplo: Gráfico de Pareto\" src=\"assets/img/501defb20a2a0db0.svg\" style=\"max-width:100%;\" /></p>\n<p><strong>Excel 365</strong>: steps already described above (<em>Insert → Charts → Insert Statistic Chart → Histogram → Pareto</em>), with the Reason and Count columns selected. Excel sorts the bars and draws the cumulative-percentage line automatically.</p>\n<p><strong>Power BI (DAX)</strong> — measures over a <code>NegativasSinistros[Motivo, Contagem]</code> table, used in a combo chart (columns sorted by descending count + cumulative-percentage line):</p>\n<div class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"dax\"><div class=\"codebar\"><span class=\"lang\">DAX</span><button class=\"btn-copy\" type=\"button\">Copiar</button></div><pre><code class=\"language-dax\">Total Geral = CALCULATE ( SUM ( NegativasSinistros[Contagem] ), ALL ( NegativasSinistros ) )\n\n% Acumulado =\nVAR RankAtual =\n    RANKX ( ALL ( NegativasSinistros[Motivo] ), CALCULATE ( SUM ( NegativasSinistros[Contagem] ) ), , DESC )\nVAR SomaAcumulada =\n    SUMX (\n        FILTER (\n            ALL ( NegativasSinistros[Motivo] ),\n            RANKX ( ALL ( NegativasSinistros[Motivo] ), CALCULATE ( SUM ( NegativasSinistros[Contagem] ) ), , DESC ) &lt;= RankAtual\n        ),\n        CALCULATE ( SUM ( NegativasSinistros[Contagem] ) )\n    )\nRETURN DIVIDE ( SomaAcumulada, [Total Geral] )\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Counts, proportions, and cumulative percentage by rank are exactly the kind of calculation DAX solves natively and well — this isn't a case of \"Power BI just visualizes\" (see the DAX limits noted in this unit's introduction).</p>\n<div class=\"codetabs\"><div class=\"ct-bar\" role=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ct-tab on\" role=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\" id=\"ct9420468-t0\">Python</button></div><div class=\"ct-pane\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"ct9420468-t0\"><div class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"python\"><div class=\"codebar\"><span class=\"lang\">Python</span><button class=\"btn-copy\" type=\"button\">Copiar</button></div><pre><code class=\"language-python\">import pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\ndados = {\n    \"Motivo\": [\n        \"Sinistro duplicado\", \"Prazo de envio\", \"Beneficiário não encontrado\",\n        \"Info. insuficiente\", \"Serviço não coberto\", \"Necessidade médica\",\n        \"Data do serviço\",\n    ],\n    \"Contagem\": [18012, 13245, 10215, 4548, 2154, 1423, 526],\n}\ndf = pd.DataFrame(dados).sort_values(\"Contagem\", ascending=False).reset_index(drop=True)\ndf[\"Percentual\"] = df[\"Contagem\"] / df[\"Contagem\"].sum() * 100\ndf[\"PercentualAcumulado\"] = df[\"Percentual\"].cumsum()\nprint(df.round(1).to_string(index=False))\n\nfig, ax1 = plt.subplots(figsize=(8, 4.5))\nax1.bar(df[\"Motivo\"], df[\"Contagem\"], color=\"#00688f\")\nax1.set_ylabel(\"Contagem\")\nax1.tick_params(axis=\"x\", rotation=45)\nax2 = ax1.twinx()\nax2.plot(df[\"Motivo\"], df[\"PercentualAcumulado\"], color=\"#16232a\", marker=\"o\")\nax2.axhline(80, color=\"gray\", linestyle=\"--\", linewidth=1)\nax2.set_ylabel(\"% acumulado\")\nax2.set_ylim(0, 105)\nplt.title(\"Pareto — Motivos de Negativa de Sinistros Médicos\")\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()</code></pre></div></div></div>\n<div class=\"codetabs\"><div class=\"ct-bar\" role=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ct-tab on\" role=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\" id=\"ct69046969-t0\">Python</button></div><div class=\"ct-pane\" role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"ct69046969-t0\"><div class=\"codeblock\" data-lang=\"python\"><div class=\"codebar\"><span class=\"lang\">Python</span><button class=\"btn-copy\" type=\"button\">Copiar</button></div><pre><code class=\"language-python\">%%R\nlibrary(qcc)\n\ncontagem &lt;- c(18012, 13245, 10215, 4548, 2154, 1423, 526)\nnames(contagem) &lt;- c(\"Duplicado\", \"Prazo de envio\", \"Beneficiario nao encontrado\",\n                      \"Info insuficiente\", \"Nao coberto\", \"Necessidade medica\",\n                      \"Data do servico\")\n\npc &lt;- pareto.chart(contagem, main = \"Pareto - Negativas de Sinistros Medicos\",\n                    ylab = \"Contagem\", ylab2 = \"Percentual acumulado\")\nprint(pc)</code></pre></div></div></div>\n<p><strong>Minitab</strong> (current version — check minitab.com): paste the Reason column into C1 and the Count column into C2, then <strong>Stat → Quality Tools → Pareto Chart</strong>. Under <em>Defect or attribute data in</em>, select C1; under <em>Frequencies in</em>, select C2 → OK. Minitab sorts the bars and overlays the cumulative-percentage line automatically, with R's <code>qcc</code> package being the closest statistical relative to this same function.</p>\n<p><em>Verification note: all five methods should converge on the same result — the top three reasons (Duplicate claim, Timely filing, Beneficiary not found) add up to ≈82.7% of the denials. Excel, DAX, and Minitab are checked manually against the table printed by the Python and R cells above.</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"voice-of-the-customer\">Voice of the Customer</h3>\n<p>Voice of the Customer, or VOC, is a fundamental concept across many quality programs. Quality's goal is to make a better, more consistent product. One way to know you've reached that goal is that your customers will be more consistently satisfied. The only way to reach that goal is to seek customer feedback, making VOC data essential to collect before, during, and after improvement projects.</p>\n<p>Successful VOC programs are proactive and constant in their desire for feedback, and technology makes it possible to seek customer feedback in various ways. A few methods for capturing feedback include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Phone, mail, email, or online surveys</li>\n<li>In-person or online focus groups</li>\n<li>Interviews</li>\n<li>Beta or user testing</li>\n<li>Feedback forms</li>\n<li>Customer complaints</li>\n<li>Interaction on social media or the website</li>\n<li>Reviews</li>\n<li>Forums</li>\n</ul>\n<p>VOC can be sought as a way to clarify needs and wants, clarify specific problems with a process, or as a regular part of improvement, customer service, and marketing agendas.</p>\n<h4 id=\"building-a-voc-campaign\">Building a VOC Campaign</h4>\n<p>Asking the right questions, the right way, helps you build powerful VOC campaigns that provide usable data for Six Sigma teams. We'll talk about two specific types of VOC campaigns in this section: general customer feedback and specific customer feedback.</p>\n<p><strong>General Feedback</strong></p>\n<p>General customer feedback is usually gathered through feedback forms, customer complaint logs, and passively collecting information via websites or social media. Through these methods, organizations are generally taking a general temperature reading: are customers satisfied overall, dissatisfied overall, and is there any direction as to the cause of customer sentiment?</p>\n<p>Pick up a feedback form at any fast-food restaurant, or pull up the online survey often linked on a receipt, and you'll get a good sense of the kind of information sought in general VOC campaigns.</p>\n<p>Kroger, a U.S. grocery chain, includes a link to a survey on most of its receipts. The survey first asks for the date, time, and an entry code from the receipt. This helps the company know where and when a person shopped, so they can attribute the feedback to the right location and staff.</p>\n<p>Next, Kroger's survey asks which areas of the store the person shopped in. The rest of the survey asks specific questions about each store area the person visited, including:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>What was the overall satisfaction with the store?</li>\n<li>What was the customer's satisfaction with:</li>\n<li>Staff friendliness</li>\n<li>Prices</li>\n<li>Service</li>\n<li>Cleanliness</li>\n<li>Item availability</li>\n<li>Weekly deals</li>\n<li>Ease of navigation</li>\n<li>Brand quality</li>\n<li>Checkout times</li>\n<li>Whether the customer is likely to recommend the store to someone else in the next 30 days.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These questions are designed to gauge customers' overall sentiment about the store's critical quality elements. Understanding your critical to quality factors, or CTQs, matters for designing a strong VOC campaign. We'll cover CTQs further in Chapter 6.</p>\n<p>General VOC feedback is often used as a smoke alarm. A smoke alarm is designed to alert individuals at a business, home, or other building that a fire might be possible. Smoke alarms are set at a sensitive level, so they go off when smoke is present and the people inside the building can act. Often, the alarm and early action save lives and can even reduce the damage tied to a possible fire.</p>\n<p>VOC data can work the same way. If the numbers suddenly shift in a particular area, an organization knows it should look further into the issue. It's an indicator that a problem might exist; early investigation and action can help keep problems from growing bigger or more expensive. For example, if a particular Kroger store always scored high on cleanliness, and the numbers dropped consistently over a month, store management might need to revisit maintenance and cleaning training.</p>\n<p>General VOC feedback results are also used at some organizations as a quality indicator for certain employees. Sales and service teams are often rewarded financially and in other ways for high customer satisfaction scores. This also increases employee motivation and satisfaction.</p>\n<p><strong>Specific Feedback</strong></p>\n<p>Sometimes organizations want specific feedback about an issue, product, or idea. The same tools used in general feedback campaigns can be used in specific campaigns, but you can also tailor the VOC tool to the need. If you want feedback on a new app, you might use beta testing. If you want to test a product, idea, or marketing campaign, an in-person or online focus group might be best.</p>\n<p>For specific feedback, you need to ask specific questions. This is especially true if you're seeking additional information or clarification on general feedback. For example, if Kroger saw a problem with cleanliness ratings, it might want more information about how and where customers notice a lack of cleanliness. Without additional feedback, managers might put staff to work mopping the floor more, when customers actually felt the store was dirty because of inadequate lighting or because shelves were stocked sloppily.</p>\n<h4 id=\"selecting-the-right-voc-tools\">Selecting the Right VOC Tools</h4>\n<p>Getting the right kind of feedback — and keeping costs and timelines within budget — requires selecting the right VOC tool for your project. The table below ranks each tool by relative cost and provides a few brief pros and cons.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Tool</th>\n<th>Cost</th>\n<th>Benefits</th>\n<th>Drawbacks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Feedback form</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>Collects lots of data from various sources; can be geared toward numeric data for easier analysis</td>\n<td>An individual has to decide to leave feedback, which skews results toward people with strong opinions one way or another</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Phone survey</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Can randomly select, allowing conclusions to be drawn about the entire population</td>\n<td>Requires many labor hours; customers might be bothered by unwanted calls</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mail survey</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Can randomly select; lower-cost alternative to phone or in-person surveys</td>\n<td>The customer needs to return the survey for it to count; since many people don't, more surveys need to be sent to get a statistical sample</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Social media</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>Ongoing ability to seek feedback; ability to ask questions quickly; possibly the least expensive VOC option</td>\n<td>Requires an established follower base; depends on followers and fans, meaning you're asking for feedback from people who already favor your brand in some way</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>In-person focus groups</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Lets moderators seek deeper answers or feedback immediately</td>\n<td>Limits the data set to local customers or those willing to travel; the data can't be used to make assumptions about the general population; customers might be less likely to be honest face to face with interviewers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Online focus groups</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>Lets moderators seek deeper answers immediately; requires no travel, and you can reach customers around the world</td>\n<td>The data can't be used to make assumptions about the general population</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>User or beta testing</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Provides feedback on a specific product, service, or process</td>\n<td>Takes time and requires experienced users or testers</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n\n<h4 id=\"the-likert-scale\">The Likert Scale</h4>\n<p>When designing your own VOC tool, keep in mind how you plan to use the information gathered. If you want to enter data into statistical analysis software to test hypotheses or build visual charts, then you need to ask questions that generate real data points that can be statistically analyzed. A popular way to do this is with a Likert Scale.</p>\n<p>Using a Likert Scale, you'd phrase every question so it's answered through a 5-point rating. The rating can take various forms, but most commonly it's some variation of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Strongly agree</li>\n<li>Agree</li>\n<li>Neutral</li>\n<li>Disagree</li>\n<li>Strongly disagree</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The answers are coded with numbers when the data is entered into statistical software. For example, a \"strongly agree\" answer might be coded as 10. \"Agree\" would be 7, \"neutral\" 5, \"disagree\" 3, and \"strongly disagree\" 1. Using numeric data, you can easily build charts and run deeper statistical analysis, covered in future chapters.</p>\n<h3 id=\"basic-metrics\">Basic Metrics</h3>\n<p>We introduced a few ideas about Six Sigma metrics in Chapter 2, when we talked about sigma level and defects per million opportunities, or DPMO. Metrics are used extensively when applying Six Sigma to processes and improvements, requiring anyone working in a Six Sigma setting to be familiar with them.</p>\n<h4 id=\"defects-per-million-opportunities-dpmo\">Defects Per Million Opportunities (DPMO)</h4>\n<p>Many Six Sigma metrics come with an equation, just like standard deviation. For example, the equation for DPMO is:</p>\n<p><strong>(number of defects in a sample / defect opportunities in the sample) × 1,000,000</strong></p>\n<p>For example, if a mail-order retailer examines the quality of its order process, it might sample forms filled out by customer service representatives. If each form has 10 fields, then there are 10 opportunities for error on each form. If the retailer reviews 90 forms, then there are 10 × 90, or 900, total opportunities for error.</p>\n<p>During the review, the retailer finds 2 errors, or defects. To calculate DPMO, the calculation would be as follows:</p>\n<p>(2/900) × 1,000,000 = 2,222 defects per million opportunities.</p>\n<h4 id=\"defects-per-unit-dpu\">Defects Per Unit (DPU)</h4>\n<p>DPU is a measure of how many defects exist relative to the number of units tested. DPU is concerned with total defects, and a unit can have more than one defect. The formula for DPU is:</p>\n<p><strong>Number of defects found / number of units in the sample</strong></p>\n<p>For example, if a publisher printed 1,000 books and pulled 50 books for quality checks, it might be looking for the following defects:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Incorrect printing</li>\n<li>Incorrect alignment</li>\n<li>Missing pages</li>\n<li>Loose binding</li>\n<li>Torn cover</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Out of 50 books, the publisher finds:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>3 books with missing pages</li>\n<li>1 book with missing pages and a torn cover</li>\n<li>2 books with loose binding</li>\n<li>1 book with incorrect printing and incorrect alignment</li>\n</ul>\n<p>There are 9 errors total, since two books had two defects each.</p>\n<p>DPU is calculated by dividing the defects by the number of units sampled. In this case, 9/50 = 0.18.</p>\n<p>DPU provides an average quality level — it tells you how many defects, on average, each unit might have. In this case, that's 0.18 defects on average.</p>\n<h4 id=\"first-time-yield-fty\">First Time Yield (FTY)</h4>\n<p>First time yield is the ratio between units produced and units that production was attempted for. For example, if you put 12 cookies in the oven, but only 10 come out edible, then you didn't produce 12 cookies.</p>\n<p>The formula for FTY is:</p>\n<p><strong>Number of good units produced / number of units that entered the process</strong></p>\n<p>In the cookie example, FTY is 10/12, or 0.833.</p>\n<p>Most products or services are created through multiple processes; you multiply each process's FTY to calculate an overall FTY. For example, consider the following process chain:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>100 units enter process A and 95 units come out.</li>\n<li>95 units enter process B and 85 good units are obtained.</li>\n<li>85 units enter process C and 80 good units come out.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>FTY would be calculated as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>95/100 = 0.95 = FTY for process A</li>\n<li>85/95 = 0.89 = FTY for process B</li>\n<li>80/85 = 0.94 = FTY for process C</li>\n<li>0.95 × 0.89 × 0.94 = 0.79</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The process's overall FTY is 0.79.</p>\n<h4 id=\"rolled-throughput-yield-rty\">Rolled Throughput Yield (RTY)</h4>\n<p>Rolled throughput yield, or RTY, provides a probability that a unit produced by a process is defect-free. One of the key differences between RTY and basic yield or first time yield is that RTY factors in whether rework was needed to generate the final unit count. That's a valuable concern, because organizations don't always think about the rework inherent to a process, which means they often measure a process and consider it successful even when muda is present.</p>\n<p>RTY is calculated similarly to FTY, but factors in rework. If process A in the FTY example hit a yield of 95 only because someone reworked five items to make them good, then RTY calculations add five instances of rework into the ratio. The formula is:</p>\n<p><strong>(Number of units entering − (scrap + rework)) / number of units entering the process</strong></p>\n<p>For process A: (100 − (5+5))/100 = 90/100 = 0.9</p>\n<p>Consider the following process chain:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>100 units enter process A. Five are scrapped, 5 are reworked, and a total of 95 are produced.</li>\n<li>95 units enter process B. Ten are scrapped, 5 are reworked, and a total of 85 are produced.</li>\n<li>85 units enter process C. Five are scrapped, 15 are reworked, and a total of 80 are produced.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>RTY is calculated as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>100 − (5 + 5) = 90, 90/100 = 0.9 RTY for A</li>\n<li>95 − (10 + 5) = 80, 80/95 = 0.84 RTY for B</li>\n<li>85 − (5 + 15) = 65, 65/85 = 0.76 RTY for C</li>\n<li>0.9 × 0.84 × 0.76 = 0.574</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The process's overall RTY is 0.574, a much lower rate than when you look only at FTY. RTY doesn't provide an indication of final output or sales, but a low RTY indicates there's waste in the process in the form of rework.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>See for yourself</strong>\nA government agency processes assistance requests for local families. The process for each request includes:\n- A representative enters the family's information into a computer system\n- A separate employee reviews the information and uses an income scale to determine whether the family is eligible for any assistance\n- The second employee sends the family a letter informing them of their assistance options</p>\n<p>Every March request and customer feedback item was reviewed, and the team found the following information:\n- 643 families sought assistance in March\n- 3 families couldn't complete the application process because the representative took too long to help them\n- 50 requests couldn't be routed to the second employee because of incomplete information\n- 45 requests didn't have complete information at first, but that information was received later\n- The second employee was able to process every complete request they received\n- Of the total letters sent to families, 10 contained incorrect information</p>\n<p>Calculate the FTY and RTY for this process.\n</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<h3 id=\"2026-technical-review-chapter-8\">2026 technical review — Chapter 8</h3>\n<p><strong>Status:</strong> Chapter-by-chapter review completed: technical content kept where it remains valid in 2026; references and terminology were verified, and the chapter remains in the training path.</p>", "toc": [{"l": 3, "t": "Standard Deviation", "id": "standard-deviation"}, {"l": 4, "t": "Calculating Standard Deviation for Population Data", "id": "calculating-standard-deviation-for-population-data"}, {"l": 4, "t": "Calculating Standard Deviation with Sample Data", "id": "calculating-standard-deviation-with-sample-data"}, {"l": 4, "t": "Standard Deviation in Excel", "id": "standard-deviation-in-excel"}, {"l": 4, "t": "Why Calculate Standard Deviation?", "id": "why-calculate-standard-deviation"}, {"l": 4, "t": "Worked example across five approaches: Standard Deviation", "id": "worked-example-across-five-approaches-standard-deviation"}, {"l": 3, "t": "The Pareto Principle", "id": "the-pareto-principle"}, {"l": 4, "t": "Building a Pareto Chart in Excel 365", "id": "building-a-pareto-chart-in-excel-365"}, {"l": 4, "t": "Uses for Pareto Charts", "id": "uses-for-pareto-charts"}, {"l": 4, "t": "Worked example across five approaches: Pareto Chart", "id": "worked-example-across-five-approaches-pareto-chart"}, {"l": 3, "t": "Voice of the Customer", "id": "voice-of-the-customer"}, {"l": 4, "t": "Building a VOC Campaign", "id": "building-a-voc-campaign"}, {"l": 4, "t": "Selecting the Right VOC Tools", "id": "selecting-the-right-voc-tools"}, {"l": 4, "t": "The Likert Scale", "id": "the-likert-scale"}, {"l": 3, "t": "Basic Metrics", "id": "basic-metrics"}, {"l": 4, "t": "Defects Per Million Opportunities (DPMO)", "id": "defects-per-million-opportunities-dpmo"}, {"l": 4, "t": "Defects Per Unit (DPU)", "id": "defects-per-unit-dpu"}, {"l": 4, "t": "First Time Yield (FTY)", "id": "first-time-yield-fty"}, {"l": 4, "t": "Rolled Throughput Yield (RTY)", "id": "rolled-throughput-yield-rty"}, {"l": 3, "t": "2026 technical review — Chapter 8", "id": "2026-technical-review-chapter-8"}], "stats": {"code": 6, "fig": 5, "words": 6152}, "new": false});
